Note that I didn't say the _language_ was abstract (nor
non-abstract) but rather that abstract thinking would
be easier for one who can think in, say, German, because of
a particular properity that German has, namely one can 
string endless batches of words together into one big noun
and do this _naturally_ whenever one needs to name a new
concept.  

There is no such thing as a "non-abstract" language.  The philologists
tell us that our picture of the birth of language as a concrete
thing growing more abstract is false.  We didn't start with 
"Rock" "Bird" "Og bang bird with rock" and then work our
way up to more complex ideas.  They tell us there is no way to
separate the abstract elements in language from the concrete ones.
As soon as Og was able to express the banging of the bird with
the rock, he was able to express the frustration he was having
with his wife as "It's like banging my head against a cave wall."


